<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tamaulipas</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tamaulipas is a state of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a>, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a>, with Ciudad Victoria as its capital. Colonised from 1748 as the Spanish province of Nuevo Santander, it became a state of independent Mexico in 1824. Its southern part lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huasteca">Huasteca</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tamaulipas.jpg" alt="Tamaulipas" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: panza.rayada · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:El_Bernalito,_en_el_valle_abajo_de_la_Cuesta_de_Llera_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tamaulipas, officially the Free and Sovereign State of Tamaulipas, is one of the 32 federated entities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and is divided into 43 municipalities. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuevo-leon">Nuevo León</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-luis-potosi">San Luis Potosí</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a>, and has a 370 km stretch of the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> along the Río Bravo, known in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a>. Its largest cities include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reynosa">Reynosa</a>, Matamoros, Nuevo Laredo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tampico">Tampico</a> and Mante. The name derives from Tamaholipa, a Huastec term whose prefix tam- means place where; the meaning of holipa is not agreed, though high hills is a common interpretation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area has been inhabited for at least 8,000 years, originally by the nomadic Chichimec and the sedentary Huastec, together with hunter-gatherer and fishing peoples. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> subjugated its inhabitants only gradually; the first permanent Spanish settlement was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tampico">Tampico</a> in 1554. Franciscan missionaries and widespread ranching followed, forcing native peoples from their lands. In the mid-18th century the region became a separate province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> called Nuevo Santander, and towns along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laredo">Laredo</a>, Mier, Camargo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reynosa">Reynosa</a>, were founded under Escandón as a defensive line.</p><p>In 1824 Tamaulipas was one of the 19 founder states of the United Mexican States. The Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, created in 1836, claimed northern Tamaulipas, and after the Mexican–American War the state lost more than a quarter of its territory in 1848 through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-guadalupe-hidalgo">Treaty of Guadalupe Hidalgo</a>. Its capital, Aguayo, was renamed Ciudad Victoria in honour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalupe-victoria">Guadalupe Victoria</a>, the first president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. The last French soldiers left the state in 1866, before the fall of Emperor Maximilian's empire in 1867. Growth followed, especially after the railroad reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tampico">Tampico</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ciudad Victoria is the capital and a significant educational centre. In the north the economy shares much with that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> and includes many maquiladoras, while the south rests on petrochemical industries around Altamira and Ciudad Madero and on the ports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tampico">Tampico</a> and Altamira. The Tropic of Cancer crosses the southern part of the municipality of Victoria. The state anthem, Himno a Tamaulipas, was composed in 1926.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tamaulipas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Mexico Apache communities live in Sonora, Chihuahua, Coahuila and <b>Tamaulipas</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/apache">Apache · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Huastec separated from other Maya speakers well over two thousand years ago and settled the lowlands of northern Veracruz, San Luis Potosí and <b>Tamaulipas</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/huastec-chiefdoms">Huastec chiefdoms</a></li><li><blockquote>With Mexican independence in 1821 the province passed to Mexico and in 1824 became the state of <b>Tamaulipas</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/nuevo-santander">Nuevo Santander</a></li><li><blockquote>Opposition to the Centralist Republic of Mexico ran deep in the northern frontier states of <b>Tamaulipas</b>, Nuevo León and Coahuila.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-the-rio-grande">Republic of the Rio Grande</a></li></ul></section>
